---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';
---
<Base title="Affiliate disclosure" description="How this site makes money, which links are commissioned, and what that does and does not change about what gets published.">
<div class="wrap"><Breadcrumbs trail={[{ label: 'Affiliate disclosure', href: '/affiliate-disclosure/' }]} /></div>
<section class="wrap" style="padding:1.5rem 1.25rem 0">
  <p class="eyebrow">Disclosure</p>
  <h1 style="max-width:22ch">How this site makes money</h1>
  <p style="max-width:46ch;font-size:1.12rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:1.2rem 0 1.8rem">
    Some links here earn a commission. That is worth stating plainly rather than burying, because a
    site that publishes cost figures and also earns from software vendors has an obvious conflict to
    manage — and you are entitled to know how it is managed.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      Some outbound links to software and services are affiliate links. If you follow one and
      subscribe, this site may receive a commission from that company. It costs you nothing and it
      does not change the price you pay.
    </p>

    <h2>What a commission does not buy</h2>
    <p>
      No company can pay for a recommendation, a rating, a placement, or the removal of a criticism.
      Where a product has a weakness that matters, it is stated whether or not there is a commercial
      relationship with that company. Where published figures about a product disagree across
      sources, the disagreement is shown rather than resolved in the vendor's favour.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pricing quoted on this site is what was published on the vendor's own materials on the date
      shown. It is not negotiated, discounted or supplied by the vendor for publication.
    </p>

    <h2>Advertising</h2>
    <p>
      This site may also display advertising served by third-party networks. Those advertisements are
      selected by the network rather than by us, and their presence is not an endorsement. Advertisers
      have no input into editorial content.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this matters more here than on most sites</h2>
    <p>
      A recurring theme across these guides is that a published figure is only as good as the party
      publishing it — and that most cost estimates in this industry come from firms selling the
      remedy. That criticism applies to this site too. The defence is not that we are unusually
      virtuous; it is that every figure carries a source and a retrieval date, so you can check it
      against the primary source yourself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where a figure here is wrong, it goes in the <a href="/corrections/">corrections log</a> with
      the date. The <a href="/editorial-standards/">editorial standards</a> page sets out how sources
      are selected and how disputes between them are handled.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      If you want to know whether a specific link is commissioned, ask. The
      <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> is the route, and the answer will be a straight yes or no.
    </p>

    <p class="note">
      {SITE.name} is an independent publication. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored
      by Amazon, Walmart, eBay, Etsy, TikTok, AliExpress, Shopify, or any of the software vendors
      discussed. All trademarks belong to their respective owners.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
